---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Basics 1 — Python Basics: Preparing to Build Your Own MCP Server"
  description="Skip a full Python course — only learn the small slice you'll actually use to build your own MCP Server: variables, strings, if, list, for, functions, return. Just enough to read the Python code in your first MCP server."
  lang="en"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container python-basics">
      <a class="back-link" href="/">&larr; Back to Home</a>

      <header class="detail-header">
        <h1 class="detail-title">The Only Python You Need Before Building an MCP</h1>
        <p class="detail-subtitle">
          We've come a long way: you've seen how to plug in off-the-shelf MCPs, how to shrink the tool surface, and now you're getting ready to build your own first MCP server. But this is when many people hit the same problem.
        </p>
        <p class="version-note">The steps and screenshots on this page were verified against <strong>Python 3.13.15</strong>. Your version may be newer; small differences on screen are normal.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          I roughly get MCP, <strong>but I'm not familiar with Python.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That's totally normal. Especially if you don't usually write code, seeing things like <code>def</code>, <code>return</code>, <code>Path("notes")</code>, or <code>@mcp.tool()</code> can make you stop and pause.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So the goal here <strong>isn't to turn you into a Python engineer</strong>. It's just one thing:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card highlight-card--strong">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>Get you just enough Python to read your "first self-built MCP server."</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In other words, we won't learn an entire Python course — <strong>only the small slice you'll actually use next</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: What is Python -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">What Is Python</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Python is a programming language. You can think of a programming language as <strong>"a way to talk to the computer"</strong>, and Python is one of the more readable, beginner-friendly options. People use it for many things, such as:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Small automation tools</li>
        <li>Data processing</li>
        <li>Web backends</li>
        <li>AI and machine learning</li>
        <li>Writing scripts</li>
        <li>Quick prototyping</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The MCP server you're about to build is a great fit for Python. <strong>Not because it's the flashiest language, but because it tends to read closer to plain English and is well-suited for a first version.</strong> For example, this one line:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>print("Hello Python")</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Just means "print out <code>Hello Python</code>."
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          So Python isn't really the main character here — it's more like material. <strong>When you build an MCP server, Python is the tool in your hand.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Install Python -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">First, Install Python Itself</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Installing Python takes two things: <strong>the installer from python.org</strong>, and <strong>PowerShell</strong>, which Windows already ships with. <strong>You don't need to install an editor first.</strong> None of this needs administrator rights either — your own account is enough.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Step 1: Download the installer from python.org</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Click <strong>Downloads</strong> → <strong>View the full list of downloads</strong>. Once you're on the downloads page, scroll down to the <strong>Files</strong> table, and note that <strong>the big yellow button in the Windows box is now "Download Python install manager", which is not the installer we want</strong> — so pick the <strong>Windows installer (64-bit)</strong> row marked <strong>Recommended</strong> instead, choosing the <strong>3.13</strong> series.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Python下載頁Files區塊.png"
          alt="The python.org downloads page: the Windows box holds a yellow Download Python install manager button, while the Files table below lists the Windows installer (64-bit) marked Recommended"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">What you want is the <code>Windows installer (64-bit)</code> row marked <strong>Recommended</strong> in the table — not the yellow button above it.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>The yellow button hands you a different installation flow</strong>, and the screens after it won't match this page. If what you see doesn't line up, check which one you downloaded.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Step 2: The two checkboxes on the installer's first screen</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        At the <strong>very bottom of the installer's first screen there are two checkboxes, and both need attention</strong>:
      </p>

      <div class="info-table-wrapper">
        <table class="info-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Checkbox</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>Use admin privileges when installing py.exe</code></td><td><strong>Leave it unticked</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Add python.exe to PATH</code></td><td><strong>Tick it</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Then click <strong>Install Now</strong>. The screen shows the install location <code>C:\Users\&lt;your-account&gt;\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python313</code> — that's your own user folder, which is why no administrator rights are needed.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Python安裝器第一畫面.png"
          alt="The first screen of the Python 3.13.15 installer, with the Use admin privileges when installing py.exe and Add python.exe to PATH checkboxes at the bottom"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">The two checkboxes at the bottom: leave the top one <strong>unticked</strong>, and <strong>tick</strong> <code>Add python.exe to PATH</code>.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          If you tick <code>Use admin privileges when installing py.exe</code>, the install stops and a window asks for an <strong>administrator password or PIN</strong>. On a machine you don't administer yourself — a school or company computer, say — this is where you get stuck. If it happens, <strong>cancel the install</strong>, go back to the first screen, untick that option, and run it again.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/UAC要求管理員密碼.png"
          alt="The Windows User Account Control window asking for an administrator username and password before the installation can continue"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Ticking that option gets you this dialog — it wants an <strong>administrator's</strong> password, not your own. (This screenshot comes from the Chinese-language run of the same install.)</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Step 3: The completion screen</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>Setup was successful</code> means you're done — just hit <strong>Close</strong>.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Python安裝完成畫面.png"
          alt="The Python setup completion screen showing Setup was successful, with a shield-marked Disable path length limit link and a Close button below"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Once you see <code>Setup was successful</code>, click <strong>Close</strong>.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The <strong>Disable path length limit</strong> line has a shield icon in front of it, which means it needs administrator rights. <strong>This course doesn't need it — don't click it.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The completion screen says you can launch Python with <code>py</code>. <strong>This course always uses <code>python</code></strong>, so ignore that sentence.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Step 4: Open a fresh window and run three checks</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        After the install finishes, <strong>search for "PowerShell" in the Start menu and open it</strong>. If one was already open before you installed, <strong>close it and open a new one</strong> — PATH only takes effect on a reload. Then run these three lines in order:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>where.exe python
python --version
python -m pip --version</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">All three matter:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><code>where.exe python</code>: lists every python on the machine. <strong>The first line must be the one you just installed</strong> (its path contains <code>AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python313</code>)</li>
        <li><code>python --version</code>: should print <code>Python 3.13.15</code></li>
        <li><code>python -m pip --version</code>: should print pip's version and path</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>A version number from <code>python --version</code> does not mean the environment works.</strong> Some machines carry a Python from another source that reports a version but has no pip — and then nothing can be installed later. In testing, <code>python --version</code> happily printed 3.12.12 while <code>python -m pip</code> reported <code>No module named pip</code>. So check all three.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          Also, if typing <code>python</code> pops open the Microsoft Store, <code>Add python.exe to PATH</code> wasn't ticked — go back to step 2 and run the installer again.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">If the first line isn't the one you just installed</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That means another Python sits ahead of it — common on machines that have had other developer tools installed. The installer can only add its path at the "user" level, while the ones ahead of it usually live at the "system" level.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>You don't need to touch any system settings.</strong> Run this single line in the PowerShell window you have open, to push the new Python to the front for now:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>$env:Path = "$env:LocalAppData\Programs\Python\Python313;$env:LocalAppData\Programs\Python\Python313\Scripts;" + $env:Path</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Then run the three lines above again to confirm.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/where查詢python排序.png"
          alt="PowerShell: where.exe python first lists a different Python at the top; after running the PATH adjustment, the Python313 path moves to the first line"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">Top half is before — the first line is a different Python. Bottom half is after running that line: <code>Python313</code> comes first, and the two remaining checks pass.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          That line <strong>only affects the window you're in</strong>; close it and the change is gone. If you close PowerShell partway through the steps below, just run it again.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Before you start: leave system32</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        When PowerShell opens from the Start menu it starts in <code>C:\WINDOWS\system32</code>, and <strong>that folder doesn't let ordinary users create files</strong>. Switch to your own folder first:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>cd $HOME</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The prompt changes from <code>PS C:\WINDOWS\system32&gt;</code> to <code>PS C:\Users\&lt;your-account&gt;&gt;</code>.
      </p>

      <!-- Section: Create first Python file -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Create Your First Python File</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <strong>Writing Python doesn't require a particular editor.</strong> PowerShell can create the file and run it — four lines and you're done.
      </p>

      <ol class="setup-list">
        <li class="setup-item">
          <span class="setup-num">1</span>
          <div class="setup-body">
            <p class="setup-text">Create a folder to practise in:</p>
            <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>New-Item -ItemType Directory -Path "$HOME\mcp-test" -Force</code></pre>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="setup-item">
          <span class="setup-num">2</span>
          <div class="setup-body">
            <p class="setup-text">Move into it:</p>
            <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>cd "$HOME\mcp-test"</code></pre>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="setup-item">
          <span class="setup-num">3</span>
          <div class="setup-body">
            <p class="setup-text">Create <code>hello.py</code> with a single <code>print</code> in it:</p>
            <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>'print("Hello Python")' | Set-Content hello.py -Encoding ASCII</code></pre>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="setup-item">
          <span class="setup-num">4</span>
          <div class="setup-body">
            <p class="setup-text">Run it:</p>
            <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>python hello.py</code></pre>
            <p class="setup-text setup-text--muted">If you're on macOS and <code>python</code> doesn't respond, try <code>python3 hello.py</code>.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If the screen shows:
      </p>
      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>Hello Python</code></pre>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        That means your first Python program ran successfully.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          <strong>When you use <code>Set-Content</code> to create a file containing Chinese, you must add <code>-Encoding UTF8</code>.</strong> Without it, Windows PowerShell falls back to its default encoding and the Chinese comes out as mojibake. The <code>hello.py</code> above is pure English, so <code>-Encoding ASCII</code> is enough — but the "build your own MCP Server" pages later are full of Chinese strings (docstrings, response messages, error text), so remember to switch to <code>-Encoding UTF8</code> when creating those.
        </p>
        <p class="callout-text">
          Also, <strong>don't create files with Notepad</strong>: it may decide to save them with a <code>.txt</code> ending, and File Explorer hides extensions by default, so everything looks perfectly fine.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          This step matters. Because when we build an MCP Server later, <strong>we'll really just be writing a Python file and letting Gemini CLI launch it.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Section: Install the MCP package -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">The Last Step: Install the MCP Package</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        While you're here, install the package you'll need later. <strong>You won't use it yet</strong>, but getting it in place now means the install won't be what holds you up when you build your own MCP Server.
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>python -m pip install "mcp[cli]&gt;=1.0,&lt;2.0"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This pulls in thirty-odd dependencies and takes one to five minutes depending on your connection — <strong>a stalled-looking screen is normal</strong>. Once it finishes, verify:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>python -m pip show mcp
python -c "from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP; print('ok')"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>pip show</code> should report <code>Version: 1.29.0</code> (or another 1.x release), and the last line should print <code>ok</code>.
      </p>

      <figure class="figure">
        <img
          class="figure__img"
          src="/learning/agent/Python環境驗證成功.png"
          alt="PowerShell: pip show mcp reports Version 1.29.0, followed by the import FastMCP test command printing ok"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption class="figure__caption">What both commands look like when they work: version <code>1.29.0</code>, and <code>ok</code> at the end.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="callout-card callout-card--warn">
        <p class="callout-text">
          The <code>&gt;=1.0,&lt;2.0</code> part <strong>must not be dropped or changed</strong>. MCP SDK 2.0 removed the style this course uses, so a 2.x install turns every later code sample into an <code>ImportError</code>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Once you see <code>ok</code>, your machine is ready to build its own MCP Server.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Concept: Variables -->
      <div class="concept-header">
        <p class="concept-label">CONCEPT 01</p>
        <h2 class="concept-title">Variables: Give Data a Name</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        From here on, you'll start seeing things written like:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>course_name = "AI Tools and Applications"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>course_name</code> here is what's called a <strong>variable</strong>. You can think of a variable as:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>A name you give to a piece of data.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">For example:</p>
      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>course_name = "AI Tools and Applications"
print(course_name)</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This means: first, store the text <code>"AI Tools and Applications"</code> in <code>course_name</code>; then use <code>print(course_name)</code> to display it.
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        The benefit of variables is that <strong>if a piece of data gets reused, you don't have to retype it every time</strong>. Later, you'll see code like this:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>BASE_DIR = Path(__file__).parent
NOTES_DIR = BASE_DIR / "class_notes"
DRAFTS_DIR = BASE_DIR / "drafts"</code></pre>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          Don't be intimidated. <code>BASE_DIR</code>, <code>NOTES_DIR</code>, and <code>DRAFTS_DIR</code> are also variables — <strong>they just hold folder locations instead of a course name</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Concept: Strings -->
      <div class="concept-header">
        <p class="concept-label">CONCEPT 02</p>
        <h2 class="concept-title">Strings: Text in Python</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In Python, text wrapped in quotes is usually called a <strong>string</strong>. For example:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>title = "My understanding of MCP"
filename = "ai_agent_basics.md"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Here, <code>"My understanding of MCP"</code> is a piece of text, and so is <code>"ai_agent_basics.md"</code>. When building an MCP Server later, you'll handle this kind of text often — things like <strong>note filenames, draft titles, and error messages</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">f-string: drop a variable into a piece of text</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Python also has a very common pattern called an <strong>f-string</strong>. For example:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>{`filename = "week1.md"
message = f"Can't find {filename}. Please check the filename first."
print(message)`}</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">It will display:</p>
      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>Can't find week1.md. Please check the filename first.</code></pre>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          You can think of an f-string as: <strong>dropping a variable into a piece of text.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        When you see <code>{`{filename}`}</code>, read it as: <strong>take the contents of the <code>filename</code> variable and put them right here</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Concept: if -->
      <div class="concept-header">
        <p class="concept-label">CONCEPT 03</p>
        <h2 class="concept-title">if: Different Reactions for Different Situations</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        <code>if</code> is used to make decisions. <strong>If something is true, do one thing; if not, do another.</strong> For example:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>score = 80

if score &gt;= 60:
    print("Pass")
else:
    print("Needs more work")</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This means: if <code>score</code> is greater than or equal to 60, show "Pass." Otherwise, show "Needs more work."
      </p>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Later, when building an MCP Server, you'll also use <code>if</code> for many <strong>safety checks</strong>. For example:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>if not notes:
    return "No notes yet."</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This can be read as: <strong>if no notes were found, return "No notes yet."</strong> There's an important idea behind this:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card highlight-card--strong">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>A good tool isn't one that always succeeds — it's one that explains itself when it fails.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>If you <strong>can't find the data</strong>, don't guess</li>
        <li>If <strong>permission is insufficient</strong>, don't push through</li>
        <li>If a <strong>file doesn't exist</strong>, tell the user clearly</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Concept: list -->
      <div class="concept-header">
        <p class="concept-label">CONCEPT 04</p>
        <h2 class="concept-title">list: Put Many Things Together</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        A <code>list</code> is a way to hold lots of items. For example, we can put many class note filenames together:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>notes = ["week1.md", "week2.md", "week3.md"]
print(notes)</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Here, <code>notes</code> contains three filenames. A <code>list</code> is good for holding <strong>"many of the same kind of thing."</strong> For example:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Many notes</li>
        <li>Many titles</li>
        <li>Many filenames</li>
        <li>Many search results</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          When we build the <code>list_notes</code> tool later, we'll need exactly this — <strong>list out all the .md notes inside a folder</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Concept: for -->
      <div class="concept-header">
        <p class="concept-label">CONCEPT 05</p>
        <h2 class="concept-title">for: Handle Things One at a Time</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        If you have a sequence of data and want to handle each item one at a time, use <code>for</code>. For example:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>notes = ["week1.md", "week2.md", "week3.md"]

for note in notes:
    print(note)</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">This program will display, in order:</p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>week1.md
week2.md
week3.md</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">You can think of it like:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>The first time, <code>note</code> is <code>"week1.md"</code></li>
        <li>The second time, <code>note</code> is <code>"week2.md"</code></li>
        <li>The third time, <code>note</code> is <code>"week3.md"</code></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Later, you might see something like this:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>for path in NOTES_DIR.glob("*.md"):
    print(path.name)</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        It means the same thing: <strong>handle each .md file you find, one at a time</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Concept: Functions -->
      <div class="concept-header">
        <p class="concept-label">CONCEPT 06</p>
        <h2 class="concept-title">Functions: Wrap Up a Piece of Work</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        This next concept is important, because <strong>tools inside an MCP Server are usually written as functions</strong>. A function is:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>Wrap up a piece of work and give it a name.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">For example:</p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>def say_hello():
    return "Hello, Python"</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Here <code>def</code> <strong>defines a function</strong>, <code>say_hello</code> is the function's name, and <code>return</code> <strong>hands the result back</strong>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout-card">
        <p class="callout-text">
          One thing to note: <strong>code inside a function is indented</strong>. The spaces in front of <code>return "Hello, Python"</code> mean it belongs to the <code>say_hello</code> function.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">Letting a function take an input</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Functions can also take inputs. For example:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>{`def greet(name):
    return f"Hello, {name}"`}</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Here <code>name</code> is <strong>the data passed into the function</strong>. If <code>name</code> is <code>"Mei"</code>, the result will be:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>Hello, Mei</code></pre>

      <h3 class="sub-heading">A more advanced form you'll see later</h3>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Later, you'll see something like:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>def read_note(filename: str) -&gt; str:</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">You can read it as:</p>

      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>This is a function called <code>read_note</code></li>
        <li>It takes a <strong>filename</strong></li>
        <li>It will <strong>return a piece of text</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Concept: return -->
      <div class="concept-header">
        <p class="concept-label">CONCEPT 07</p>
        <h2 class="concept-title">return: Not Printing, But Handing the Result Back</h2>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        Many people have trouble distinguishing <code>print</code> from <code>return</code> at first.
      </p>

      <div class="diff-grid">
        <article class="diff-card">
          <p class="diff-card__name"><code>print</code></p>
          <p class="diff-card__desc"><strong>Displays</strong> something on the screen.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="diff-card diff-card--primary">
          <p class="diff-card__name"><code>return</code></p>
          <p class="diff-card__desc"><strong>Hands the result back</strong> to whoever called it.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">For example:</p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>def get_message():
    return "This is the result to hand back."</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        In casual practice, we often use <code>print</code> to inspect results. But inside an MCP Server, you'll see <code>return</code> much more often. The reason:
      </p>

      <div class="highlight-card highlight-card--strong">
        <p class="highlight-text">
          <strong>The result of an MCP tool needs to be handed back to Gemini CLI — not just printed in your own terminal.</strong>
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        So when you see this at the end of an MCP tool later:
      </p>

      <pre class="code-block code-block--py"><code>return "Draft created."</code></pre>

      <p class="detail-paragraph">
        You can read it as: <strong>this tool hands its execution result back to AI</strong>.
      </p>

      <!-- Optional appendix: VS Code -->
      <h2 class="detail-heading">Appendix: If You'd Rather Use VS Code</h2>
      <p class="detail-paragraph">
